comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-02T21:11:31.406Z","harness:claude-code","Measured convergence regime (2026-08-02, pm 2026.8.2, sandbox tracker, installed merge drivers). Union collections converge: the eleven ITEM_UNION_COLLECTION_FIELDS merge by element identity and are byte-identical in every direction and application order tested. Scalars do not: with branches A and B each rewriting description, priority, estimated_minutes and assignee, A<-B retains all four of A's values and B<-A retains all four of B's, because scalar three-way always resolves toward the merging side (preferred: ours). The merge is associative — A<-B<-C and A<-C<-B agree — so the failure is commutativity alone, and the two non-commuting directions are exactly the fleet's two: agent merges main into branch, integrator merges branch into main. Both merged workspaces report validate ok:true after git add and history-repair, and neither carries any recorded signal that the other exists. Identity has the same shape: two branches that mint one id produce one fused document with two create entries, and storage integrity only reports it when the two items had different types. Filed as pm-dlx7v7 (product), pm-1tns7o (the property suite that misses the class), pm-qx95lz (identity fusion); this decision is now blocked_by all three so the model is chosen against measurement rather than assertion."
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-14T10:20:08.634Z","harness:codex","Convergence decision experiment matrix. Evaluate field-aware Git merge, append-only operation logs, hybrid per-field CRDTs, and full CRDT state across independent-item writes, same-item union collections, conflicting scalars, concurrent lifecycle transitions, identical id creation, deletes and restores, long-offline branches, reordered merges, repeated merges, network partitions, process crashes, and partial history writes. Scale from two branches to randomized N-branch and thousand-agent simulations. Check convergence, commutativity, associativity, idempotence, causality preservation, deterministic conflict receipts, lossless recovery, history addressability, plain-text diff quality, repository growth, latency, and migration cost. A hybrid is preferable unless evidence disproves it: union or set semantics for append collections, explicit recorded conflict values for scalars, collision-resistant identity with a fusion refusal, and an append-only merge receipt that makes the projected merged state restorable. The ADR must name the exact regime where Git plus receipts is sufficient and the threshold where stronger replicated-state machinery becomes necessary."
body: "## Question\nThe product target is thousands of agents working the same workspace across many branches, merging without conflict or loss. Today that is delivered by field-aware three-way merge drivers plus history union and reanchoring. Those are correct for the divergence patterns tested. The open question is whether they remain correct — and remain conflict-free rather than merely correct — as branch count and divergence depth grow.\n\n## Tension\nConvergent replicated state buys order-independent merge by construction, which is exactly the property the merge conformance suite is being built to assert. It costs metadata weight in every record and it works against the plain-text, human-readable, git-diffable storage property that is a deliberate product commitment.\n\n## Constraint that must be respected\nStorage stays human-readable and git-native. Any convergence mechanism that turns items into opaque blobs is rejected on that ground alone, regardless of its theoretical merits.\n\n## Evidence this decision waits on\nMeasured conflict rates from the merge conformance property suite at increasing branch counts, and invariant violations found by the deterministic simulation harness. Deciding before that evidence exists would be a preference, not a decision."
